<%# "What was different?" — baseline against selection, per dimension.

    The ORDER is the requirement, not the layout. Honeycomb sorts these cards
    by how different they are; this cannot, because:

      STAT-019/024 — within-subject findings are ranked lexicographically
        (eligibility → evidence → effect magnitude → stability → quality →
        domain relevance), never by a single numeric score.
      STAT-035    — measurement and instrumentation findings take precedence
        over biological ones regardless of magnitude.

    So a source change that moves 14 readings outranks a 21 mmHg shift, and
    sorting by effect size would put them the other way round — which is the
    exact inversion STAT-035 exists to prevent. Coverage comes before both,
    because every share below is read through its denominator.

    Nothing here carries evidence strength. These are counts and shares of
    what is in the database; a Difference with a strength attached is an
    analytical artifact built by Epic 003, and this is a screen.

    Locals: profile. %>
<section class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">What was different?</h2>

  <p class="tiny muted" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem">
    <span class="legend-item">
      <span class="legend-swatch" style="background: var(--viz-baseline)"></span> baseline
      <span class="faint">(everything outside the selection)</span>
    </span>
    &nbsp;
    <span class="legend-item">
      <span class="legend-swatch" style="background: var(--viz-selection)"></span> selection
    </span>
    &nbsp;· descriptive comparison, ordered by contract precedence rather than by
    effect size. Not ranked, and not evidence.
  </p>

  <%# Selecting a value band and then reporting which dimensions differ is a
      hypothesis generator, not a finding: some of the difference exists
      BECAUSE of the selection. Honeycomb's BubbleUp has the same property and
      does not say so; a tool whose whole promise is an honest representation
      of the available evidence has to. %>
  <% if profile.conditioned_on_value? %>
    <p class="note note-warn" style="margin-bottom:0.6rem">
      This selection is conditioned on the measured value, so dimensions may
      differ <em>because</em> of how it was drawn rather than for any reason in
      the subject. Treat it as somewhere to look, never as a result.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <div class="dims">
    <%# Coverage first. A share over four readings and a share over forty look
        identical on a bar and mean entirely different things. %>
    <div class="dim">
      <div class="dim-head">
        <span class="dim-title">Coverage</span>
        <span class="dim-kind">denominator</span>
      </div>

      <div class="dim-stats">
        <span></span>
        <span class="head">base</span>
        <span class="head">sel</span>
        <% profile.coverage_stats.each do |stat| %>
          <span class="label" title="<%= stat.note %>"><%= stat.label %></span>
          <span class="value value-baseline"><%= stat.baseline %></span>
          <span class="value value-selection"><%= stat.selection %></span>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <p class="dim-note">
        Readings taken together are one measurement event, not several — repeated
        readings are not independent n (STAT-004/005/021).
      </p>
    </div>

    <%# Measurement behaviour. First-class analyzable data (STAT-003), not
        metadata about the real data. %>
    <% profile.profiles.each do |dimension| %>
      <div class="dim">
        <div class="dim-head">
          <span class="dim-title"><%= dimension.label %></span>
          <span class="dim-kind"><%= dimension.kind %></span>
          <% if dimension.filter_key && profile.filters.key?(dimension.filter_key) %>
            <%= link_to "clear", scope_path(base_path, profile.filters, remove: dimension.filter_key),
                        class: "dim-clear", data: { turbo_frame: frame_id || "_top" } %>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <%# A dimension with one value in both windows has nothing to draw two
            bars of — 100% against 100% is a sentence, not a chart, and three
            such cards side by side buried the one dimension that did move.
            "Unchanged" is still reported: an absent instrumentation change is
            a real answer, and silently dropping the card would leave a reader
            unable to tell "unchanged" from "never checked". %>
        <% if dimension.unchanged? %>
          <p class="dim-unchanged">
            <span class="dim-unchanged-mark" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
            <% if profile.filters.key?(dimension.filter_key.to_s) %>
              scoped to <strong><%= dimension.rows.first.label %></strong>
            <% else %>
              unchanged — all readings <strong><%= dimension.rows.first.label %></strong>
            <% end %>
          </p>
        <% else %>
          <% dimension.rows.each do |row| %>
            <% drillable = dimension.filter_key && row.value.present? && !profile.filters.key?(dimension.filter_key) %>
            <div class="<%= class_names("dim-row", "is-drillable" => drillable) %>">
              <div class="dim-row-head">
                <%# Clicking a value scopes the whole view to it — the half of
                    the loop the strip was missing. Folded "other" rows are not
                    drillable: they stand for several values, and filtering by a
                    bucket the operator cannot name is not a drill-down. %>
                <% label_classes = class_names("dim-row-label truncate", "mono" => row.handle.present?) %>
                <% if drillable %>
                  <%= link_to scope_path(base_path, profile.filters, add: { dimension.filter_key => row.value }),
                              class: "#{label_classes} dim-drill",
                              title: "Scope everything to #{row.label}",
                              data: { turbo_frame: frame_id || "_top" } do %>
                    <%= row.label %><span class="dim-drill-mark" aria-hidden="true">⤵</span>
                  <% end %>
                <% else %>
                  <span class="<%= label_classes %>" title="<%= row.label %>"><%= row.label %></span>
                <% end %>

                <span class="dim-row-values">
                  <span class="shares"><%= format_share(row.baseline_share) %> → <%= format_share(row.selection_share) %></span>
                  <span class="faint counts"><%= row.baseline_count %> → <%= row.selection_count %></span>
                </span>
              </div>

              <%# Two thin bars on one shared 0–100% scale. Never two scales. %>
              <div class="dim-bars">
                <div class="dim-bar dim-bar-baseline">
                  <span style="width: <%= ((row.baseline_share || 0) * 100).round(1) %>%"></span>
                </div>
                <div class="dim-bar dim-bar-selection">
                  <span style="width: <%= ((row.selection_share || 0) * 100).round(1) %>%"></span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

        <p class="dim-note">
          <%= dimension.note %>
          <span class="faint">Denominator <%= dimension.baseline_total %> / <%= dimension.selection_total %> <%= dimension.unit_label %>.</span>
          <%# No silent caps: a top-N that does not say it is a top-N reads as
              "these are all of them". %>
          <% if dimension.truncated_from %>
            <span class="faint">Showing <%= Ops::TelemetryProfileQuery::MAX_ROWS %> of
              <%= dimension.truncated_from %> values; the rest are folded into "other".</span>
          <% end %>
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# Biology last and explicitly caveated (STAT-035). %>
    <% if (summary = profile.value_summary) %>
      <div class="dim">
        <div class="dim-head">
          <span class="dim-title">Value level</span>
          <span class="dim-kind">biological</span>
        </div>

        <div class="dim-stats">
          <span></span>
          <span class="head">base</span>
          <span class="head">sel</span>
          <span class="label"><%= summary.label %></span>
          <span class="value value-baseline"><%= summary.baseline %></span>
          <span class="value value-selection"><%= summary.selection %></span>
        </div>

        <p class="dim-note">
          <%= summary.note %>
          Read the measurement dimensions first: a change in how a reading is
          taken can look exactly like a change in the subject.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
